---
import InsightArticle from '../../layouts/InsightArticle.astro';
import LogBarChart from '../../components/insights/LogBarChart.astro';
import DataNote from '../../components/insights/DataNote.astro';
import insights from '../../data/insights/2026-11-14.json';

const rawBase = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const base = rawBase.endsWith('/') ? rawBase : `${rawBase}/`;

const snapDate = new Date(insights.snapshotDate).toLocaleDateString('en-GB', { day: 'numeric', month: 'long', year: 'numeric', timeZone: 'UTC' });
const rows = insights.priceSpread.all;
const pick = (name: string) => rows.find((m) => m.name === name)!;
const money = (v: number) =>
  Number.isInteger(v) || v >= 1000 ? `$${Math.round(v).toLocaleString('en-GB')}` : `$${v.toFixed(2)}`;
const n = (v: number) => v.toLocaleString('en-GB');
const ratioNow = insights.outputInputRatio.series[insights.outputInputRatio.series.length - 1].median;

// The example job. These are assumptions chosen to show the arithmetic, not measurements.
const job = { requests: 10_000, tokensIn: 3_000, tokensOut: 500 };
const totalIn = job.requests * job.tokensIn;
const totalOut = job.requests * job.tokensOut;
const bill = (m: { input: number; output: number }) => ({
  input: (totalIn / 1_000_000) * m.input,
  output: (totalOut / 1_000_000) * m.output,
});
const models = ['Mistral Nemo', 'Claude Haiku 4.5', 'GPT-5.5', 'GPT-5.5 Pro'].map(pick);
const bills = models.map((m) => {
  const cost = bill(m);
  return { ...m, costIn: cost.input, costOut: cost.output, total: cost.input + cost.output };
});

// Four ways the same job changes, priced on one model.
const ref = pick('GPT-5.5');
const base0 = bill(ref);
const plain = base0.input + base0.output;
// Caching: a hypothetical best case. 2,560 of the 3,000 input tokens (20 blocks of 128, the unit OpenAI counts
// cached tokens in) are a repeated opening, and every request reads them at GPT-5.5's cached-input price of
// $0.50 per million tokens, a tenth of its $5 input price (OpenAI model page, read 14 November 2026).
const cachedTokens = 2_560;
const cacheRate = 0.1;
const cachedPrice = ref.input * cacheRate;
const monthlySpread = Math.round(bills[bills.length - 1].total / bills[0].total);
// Our blended price assumes one part input to three parts output: (input + 3 x output) / 4.
const blendedBill = ((ref.input + 3 * ref.output) / 4) * ((totalIn + totalOut) / 1_000_000);
const isReseller = (source: string) => /openrouter/i.test(source);
const cachedInput =
  (job.requests * ((job.tokensIn - cachedTokens) * ref.input + cachedTokens * ref.input * cacheRate)) / 1_000_000;
const withCache = cachedInput + base0.output;
// Batch: half price on the whole request.
const withBatch = plain * 0.5;
// Reasoning: 2,000 extra output tokens per request that you never see.
const reasoningTokens = 2_000;
const reasoningCost = ((job.requests * reasoningTokens) / 1_000_000) * ref.output;
const withReasoning = plain + reasoningCost;
const scenarios = [
  { label: 'As listed', total: plain, note: 'Input and output at list price' },
  { label: 'With a cached opening', total: withCache, note: `Best case: ${n(cachedTokens)} of the ${n(job.tokensIn)} input tokens repeat, and every request reads them from the cache at $${cachedPrice.toFixed(2)} per million` },
  { label: 'Sent as a batch', total: withBatch, note: '50% discount, 24-hour completion window' },
  { label: 'With reasoning switched on', total: withReasoning, note: `${n(reasoningTokens)} reasoning tokens per request, billed as output` },
];
---

<InsightArticle
  title="How to read an AI price list"
  description="A worked guide to AI model pricing: input and output tokens, reasoning, caching and batch discounts, with one example job priced on four models and four ways."
  date="2026-11-14"
  type="Explainer"
  readTime="8 min"
  snapshot={insights.snapshotDate} standfirst="An AI price list starts with two numbers per model: a price to read and a price to write. This guide takes one job, prices it on four models, then shows how reasoning, caching and batching move the bill."
>
  <p>
    The headline prices for an AI model are two numbers, both quoted per million tokens. {ref.name} is listed at
    ${ref.input} for input and ${ref.output} for output. Other rates sit beside them on the same page: a cheaper rate for
    cached input, a discount for batches, and sometimes a higher rate for very long prompts. This guide explains the parts
    of a price list in the order they affect what you pay.
  </p>

  <h2>1. Tokens, not words</h2>
  <p>
    Models count text in tokens, which are chunks of a word. OpenAI's <a href="https://help.openai.com/en/articles/4936856-what-are-tokens-and-how-to-count-them" rel="noopener">guide to tokens</a> (read on 14 November 2026) gives the rule of thumb that 100 tokens is about 75 words of English. So a million tokens is
    roughly 750,000 words, and a 1,500-word report is about 2,000 tokens.
  </p>
  <p>
    The rule is for English prose. Code, tables and other languages come out differently, so count a real sample of your own
    text with a <a href={`${base}token-counter/`}>token counter</a> before you trust an estimate.
  </p>

  <h2>2. Input and output are priced apart</h2>
  <p>
    The input price covers everything you send: your instructions, any documents, and the whole conversation so far. The
    output price covers what the model writes back. Output is usually dearer. Among models released since July, the typical
    output price is {ratioNow} times the input price, up from three times in late 2024. We looked at that in <a href={`${base}blog/output-tokens-cost-five-times-input/`}>a separate article</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    A chat application that keeps the full history does not send only your latest message. It sends the whole conversation
    again on every turn, so the input side of a long chat grows with each reply. Applications can trim or summarise the
    history to limit that.
  </p>

  <h2>3. One job on four models</h2>
  <p>
    Here is an illustrative job: {n(job.requests)} requests in a month, each sending {n(job.tokensIn)} tokens and
    getting {n(job.tokensOut)} back. That is {n(totalIn / 1_000_000)} million input tokens and {n(totalOut / 1_000_000)} million output tokens. The token counts are assumptions to show the sums. The prices are real.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <caption>
        Prices: AI Resource Hub catalogue, snapshot of {snapDate}, US dollars per million tokens. Job size is an assumption:{' '}
        {n(job.requests)} requests of {n(job.tokensIn)} tokens in and {n(job.tokensOut)} out.
      </caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Model</th><th>Price from</th><th class="num">Input price</th><th class="num">Output price</th><th class="num">Input cost</th><th class="num">Output cost</th><th class="num">Month's bill</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {bills.map((b) => (
          <tr>
            <td>{b.name} <span style="color:var(--color-text-muted)">({b.provider})</span></td>
            <td>{isReseller(b.source) ? 'OpenRouter' : "Maker's list"}</td>
            <td class="num">${b.input}</td>
            <td class="num">${b.output}</td>
            <td class="num">{money(b.costIn)}</td>
            <td class="num">{money(b.costOut)}</td>
            <td class="num"><strong>{money(b.total)}</strong></td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <LogBarChart
    title="The same month of work on four models"
    summary={`Monthly bill for the example job: ${bills.map((b) => `${b.name} ${money(b.total)}`).join(', ')}.`} ticks={[1, 10, 100, 1000, 10000]} data={bills.map((b, i) => ({ label: b.name, value: b.total, valueLabel: money(b.total), highlight: i === bills.length - 1 }))} caption={`Prices: AI Resource Hub catalogue, snapshot of ${snapDate}. US dollars. Logarithmic scale: each gridline is ten times the one before. Job size is an assumption.`}
  />

  <p>
    The same work costs about a dollar or about {money(bills[bills.length - 1].total)}, a gap of roughly {n(monthlySpread)} to
    one. That is smaller than the 6,000-fold gap in output prices that we wrote about in <a href={`${base}blog/ai-price-spread-6000x/`}>another piece</a>, because this job is mostly input and input prices sit closer together. Notice too
    that on {ref.name} the output side costs as much as the input side, even though the job sends six times more tokens than
    it receives.
  </p>

  <h2>4. The other rates on the list</h2>
  <p>
    Now keep the model fixed at {ref.name} and change how the job is run. The list price gives {money(plain)} a month.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <caption>
        Hypothetical arithmetic. {ref.name} list prices from the snapshot of {snapDate}, and its cached-input price from
        OpenAI's model page read on 14 November 2026. Token counts are assumptions. The cached row assumes every request
        reads the whole repeated opening from the cache, which is the best case.
      </caption>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>How the job is run</th><th class="num">Month's bill</th><th class="num">Against list</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {scenarios.map((s) => (
          <tr>
            <td>{s.label}<br /><span style="color:var(--color-text-muted);font-weight:400;font-size:0.8rem">{s.note}</span></td>
            <td class="num"><strong>{money(s.total)}</strong></td>
            <td class="num">{s.total === plain ? 'n/a' : `${s.total > plain ? '+' : '−'}${Number(Math.abs(((s.total - plain) / plain) * 100).toFixed(1))}%`}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p><strong>Reasoning.</strong> Some models can think before they answer. OpenAI's <a href="https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/reasoning" rel="noopener">documentation</a> (read on 14 November 2026) says those thinking tokens are not shown to you but "are billed as output tokens", and that one response can use tens of thousands. In the table, {n(reasoningTokens)} of them per request triple the bill. If you switch a reasoning mode on, measure it on a few real requests before you budget.</p>

  <p><strong>Caching.</strong> If every request starts with the same long instructions or the same document, the provider can keep that opening ready and charge much less to read it back. OpenAI's <a href="https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/prompt-caching" rel="noopener">caching guide</a> says this is on by default for supported models, and its <a href="https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/models/gpt-5.5" rel="noopener">{ref.name} page</a> lists cached input at $0.50 per million tokens, a tenth of the normal $5. Anthropic's <a href="https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/prompt-caching" rel="noopener">caching guide</a> gives a standard cached-read price of a tenth of base input, with lower rates on selected models. Writing to its cache costs 25% more than normal input at the default five-minute lifetime, and that lifetime restarts each time the cached text is used. All three pages were read on 14 November 2026. Caching helps only when the repeated text comes first and the next request arrives before the cache expires.</p>

  <p><strong>Batch.</strong> If you do not need the answer at once, you can hand over a file of requests and collect the results later. OpenAI's <a href="https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/batch" rel="noopener">Batch API guide</a> (read on 14 November 2026) gives a 50% discount for this, with a 24-hour completion window. Requests not finished in that window are cancelled, and you pay only for the ones that completed. It suits overnight jobs such as tagging a back catalogue. It is no use for a chat window.</p>

  <h2>5. Whose price is it?</h2>
  <p>
    The last thing to check is who is quoting. A model that anyone may host, such as Mistral Nemo or the Llama family, is sold
    by many hosting companies at different prices, and our tables show the cheapest one that OpenRouter lists. A model sold only by its maker is
    priced on the maker's own page, and that page can hold more than one rate: OpenAI's {ref.name} page lists a higher price
    for prompts over 272,000 input tokens and a 10% uplift for regional processing. Our own tables mix the two kinds of
    price, and the first table in this guide shows which is which. Our <a href={`${base}transparency/#source-openrouter-models`}>transparency page</a> explains where each price comes from.
  </p>
  <p>
    A single "blended" price per million tokens is a third thing again. It assumes a fixed mix of input and output. Ours
    assumes one part input to three parts output, and <a href={`${base}transparency/#calc-blended-price`}>the formula is published</a>. The example job in this guide is six parts input to one part output. Apply our blended
    price to it on {ref.name} and you get {money(blendedBill)}, against the {money(plain)} worked out above.
  </p>

  <h2>A short checklist</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Count the tokens in a real request and a real answer. Do not guess from word counts alone.</li>
    <li>Multiply by your monthly volume, input and output separately.</li>
    <li>If you use a reasoning mode, add its tokens to the output side.</li>
    <li>If your requests share a long opening, check the provider's caching rules and price the repeated part at the cached rate.</li>
    <li>If the work can wait a day, price it as a batch.</li>
    <li>Check whether the price is the maker's list price or a reseller's.</li>
    <li>Run a small real test and read the usage report before you commit.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    The <a href={`${base}calculator/`}>cost calculator</a> estimates a monthly cost from the token counts you enter, for the models it supports, and the <a href={`${base}guides/understanding-ai-pricing/`}>pricing guide</a> goes over the basics.
  </p>

  <DataNote anchor="calc-price-spread" snapshot={`${snapDate} (catalogue rebuilt ${insights.cacheGeneratedAt.slice(11, 16)} UTC)`}>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Prices.</strong> From our catalogue snapshot. Mistral Nemo and Claude Haiku 4.5 are priced from OpenRouter's
        public list; GPT-5.5 and GPT-5.5 Pro are OpenAI's own list prices, entered by hand.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The job is invented.</strong> Request counts, token counts, the share of input that repeats and the number of
        reasoning tokens are round numbers chosen to show the arithmetic. Your own figures will differ.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Discount rules are the providers' own.</strong> The caching and batch rates are quoted from each provider's
        documentation on the date shown and can change. The cached example uses {ref.name}'s cached-input price from OpenAI's model page. OpenAI's caching guide says cached tokens are reported rounded down to a multiple of 128, which is why the example uses {n(cachedTokens)}; how much of a prompt is cached also depends on request settings.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </DataNote>
</InsightArticle>
